const counter = {
  value: 0,
  addAll(values) {
    values.forEach(value => {
      this.value += value;
    });
  },
};

counter.addAll([1, 2, 3]);
console.log(counter.value);

sections.content:
**Mechanism.** A regular function creates a fresh function environment that can receive `this` from the call site. An arrow closes over the surrounding environment instead. The decision is therefore about receiver ownership, not personal style.

| Capability | Arrow function | Regular function |
|---|---|---|
| Own `this` | No; lexical capture | Yes; decided by call form |
| Own `arguments` | No | Yes |
| Usable with `new` | No | Yes, when constructable |
| Generator body | No | Yes with `function*` |
| Strong fit | Callback or closure | Method, constructor, dynamic receiver |

**Runnable example.** The method establishes the receiver and the arrow carries it into the callback. The failed constructor call shows a language boundary, not a lint preference.

```javascript
const counter = {
  value: 0,
  addLater() {
    const run = () => { this.value += 1; };
    run();
  },
};

counter.addLater();
console.log(counter.value); // 1

const ArrowPoint = (x, y) => ({ x, y });
try { new ArrowPoint(2, 3); } catch (error) {
  console.log(error instanceof TypeError); // true
}
```

**Boundary.** An arrow used as an object-literal method normally captures an outer `this`, not the object. A class-field arrow captures an instance but creates a new function per instance, unlike a prototype method.

