const makeLabel = ({ name = "Guest" } = {}) => ({ label: name });

const account = {
  name: "Mina",
  greeting() {
    return makeLabel({ name: this.name });
  },
};

console.log(makeLabel());
console.log(account.greeting());

sections.content:
**Diagnosis map.** The visible error points toward a specific language rule. An unexpected `this` value is a receiver issue; `undefined` from an object-returning callback is a body-parsing issue; a destructuring `TypeError` is usually a missing source; leaked nested mutation is a shallow-copy issue.

| Symptom | Cause | Repair |
|---|---|---|
| Method reads wrong `this` | Arrow captured outer receiver | Use method syntax or close over the intended value |
| Mapper returns `undefined` | `{}` parsed as block body | Wrap object literal in parentheses |
| Parameter destructuring throws | Whole argument is absent | Add an outer default or validate first |
| Nested value changes original | Rest copy is shallow | Copy the required nested ownership boundary |

**Runnable example.** The safe function supports an omitted object and makes its object return unambiguous.

```javascript
const makeLabel = ({ name = "Guest" } = {}) => ({ label: name });

const account = {
  name: "Mina",
  greeting() { return makeLabel({ name: this.name }); },
};

console.log(makeLabel());              // { label: 'Guest' }
console.log(account.greeting());       // { label: 'Mina' }
```

**Boundary.** Defaults should represent valid omitted input, not conceal malformed data. If `null` or a wrong type is invalid, reject it explicitly instead of adding layers of fallback syntax.
